# Docker build-context exclusions (E-256-06).
#
# The Dockerfile COPYs exactly four things: requirements.txt, src/, migrations/,
# and pyproject.toml. Everything else in the repo root is sent to the daemon as
# build context for no reason -- and some of it must never be able to enter a
# layer at all. This file is an EXCLUDE list only (no `*` + negation), so what it
# keeps out can be read off the patterns directly.
#
# ANCHORING. Docker anchors an unprefixed pattern at the context root: `data/`
# matches ./data and nothing else, and `*.pyc` matches only root-level bytecode.
# A pattern must say `**/` when it means "at any depth". Each group below states
# which it is, because a reader cannot otherwise distinguish intended anchoring
# from overlooked anchoring.
#
# CLAUDE.md Security Rules: credentials and tokens must never appear in code,
# logs, commit history, or agent output. A build context is none of those, but it
# is one careless `COPY . .` away from all of them.

# --- Credentials and secrets. Never in the context, never in a layer. ---
# RECURSIVE, and must stay that way: `COPY src/ ./src/` would carry a dotenv file
# created anywhere under src/ straight into a layer. This is the one pattern that
# must never be narrower than the COPY it guards. The root-anchored form is kept
# alongside it as belt-and-braces.
**/.env*
.env*
secrets/
proxy/

# --- Live data: the SQLite DB, backups, generated reports, raw API responses. ---
# ROOT-ANCHORED ON PURPOSE. There is exactly one data/ and one ephemeral/, both at
# the context root; a nested match would be a different directory, not this one.
data/
ephemeral/

# --- VCS. The version in pyproject.toml is static, so the build never needs it. ---
# ROOT-ANCHORED ON PURPOSE, same reason: there is exactly one repository root.
.git/
.gitignore
.githooks/

# --- Python build/run artifacts. RECURSIVE: these nest under src/, which is COPYd. ---
# Regenerated inside the image, and stale by construction -- story 03 deleted source
# whose .pyc would otherwise still ride along. A root-anchored `*.pyc` would miss
# every one of them.
#
# Docker documents `**` as matching zero or more directories, so the recursive form
# alone should also cover the root. The root-anchored forms are kept alongside it
# rather than relying on that: same belt-and-braces as the credentials group.
**/__pycache__/
__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
*.pyc
**/*.pyo
*.pyo
**/*.egg-info/
*.egg-info/

# --- Tool caches and virtualenvs. ROOT-ANCHORED ON PURPOSE. ---
# Split out of the group above: these are single top-level directories, so anchoring
# is correct for them -- but the RECURSIVE header would have over-claimed it.
.venv/
.uv-cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# --- Not needed at runtime: tests, docs, tooling, planning artifacts. ---
# ROOT-ANCHORED ON PURPOSE: each names a single top-level directory.
tests/
docs/
scripts/
epics/
reviews/
.project/
.claude/
.agents/
.codex/
.codex-home/
.devcontainer/
.mcp.json

# --- Compose/Docker files themselves are not build inputs. ---
# ROOT-ANCHORED ON PURPOSE: each names a single top-level file. A `Dockerfile` nested
# under src/ would be someone else's, and excluding it is not this file's business.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml
docker-compose*.yml.example

# --- OS noise. RECURSIVE: Finder drops these at any depth. ---
# Root twin kept, same belt-and-braces as the credentials and artifact groups.
**/.DS_Store
.DS_Store
